task: crdt-seam
agent: opus5-intent
started: 2026-09-09
branch: feature/crdt-seam
worktree: ../okay-wt-seam

why: stage 3 of specs/coordination-free.md, the last one open. The
  spec's own overview says why it should be small: okay-cache's `View`
  is built from `fold: (Option[V], Record) => Option[V]` and says "a
  cache over a log is a CONSUMER, never invalid, only BEHIND". A CRDT
  is that fold with laws. If the seam turns out to be large, the
  claim that they are the same shape was wrong.

what: `Crdt.folding` — a CRDT read as a fold — plus the law that
  matters at the log level.

  GENERIC OVER THE RECORD TYPE, deliberately, so `okay-crdt` gains NO
  dependency: the seam is a function, not a module edge. okay-cache
  depends on okay-persist; making okay-crdt depend on either to
  express `(Option[V], R) => Option[V]` would be coupling bought for
  nothing, and `View.apply` accepts any function of that shape.

THE LAW THIS STAGE IS ABOUT, and it is not one of the three from
  stage 2: a fold over records IN ANY ORDER lands on the same value.
  That is what commutativity, associativity and idempotence BUY once
  a log is involved, said in the form a `View` actually experiences —
  partitions interleave, a consumer replays, a rebuild reads
  compaction's leftovers. Stage 2 proved the algebra; this proves the
  consequence.

DISQUALIFYING EVIDENCE, registered before the work:

  - if the seam needs more than a few lines, the spec's premise ("a
    CRDT IS a fold") is wrong and I should say so rather than build a
    bridge and call the gap closed.
  - the permutation law must FAIL for a non-CRDT fold. I will check
    it against last-write-wins-by-arrival — the fold a cache would
    naively use — which is order DEPENDENT. If the law passes for
    that too, it is not testing order-independence.
  - a fold that only ever sees records in one order proves nothing:
    the test must permute, and include duplicates, because
    redelivery is the idempotence half.

predict: the seam is under ten lines and the interesting work is
  entirely in the law. Recorded so being wrong is visible.
